ading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kumiko is a Japanese woodworking technique in which thin strips of wood are interlaced to create latticed panels that incorporate intricate patterns.</li><li>These wooden strips are fit together without the use of nails or other fasteners.</li><li>Many of the geometric patterns used in kumiko designs are inspired by elements from nature.</li><li>The overlapping hexagons of the kikko pattern resemble a tortoise's shell.</li><li>The curved fan shapes of the seigaiha pattern resemble ocean waves.</li></ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to make and support a claim about the inspiration for kumiko patterns. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>The kumiko woodworking technique is used to create latticed panels with geometric designs, like overlapping hexagons, out of thin strips of wood.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>The tortoiseshell-like overlapping hexagons of the kikko pattern are inspired by wooden strips from nature.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Like the seigaiha pattern's curved fan shapes, which resemble ocean waves, many of the geometric patterns used in kumiko designs are inspired by elements from nature.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Often resembling elements from nature, kumiko patterns are formed by fitting thin strips of wood together without the use of nails.</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"26","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>The willow oak is a species of deciduous tree.</li><li>Its scientific name is <em>Quercus phellos</em>.</li><li>It is native to the Southeastern ecoregion of the US.</li></ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to indicate which ecoregion the willow oak is native to. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>The willow oak's scientific name is <em>Quercus phellos</em>.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Deciduous trees are native to several ecoregions of the US.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>The willow oak is native to the Southeastern ecoregion of the US.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>The willow oak (<em>Quercus phellos</em>) is a species of deciduous tree.</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"27","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>In music theory, the term \"key\" refers to the set of musical notes that forms the foundation of a piece of music.</li><li>In <em>Ideas Toward an Aesthetic of Music</em> (1806), German poet and composer Christian Schubart describes the mood of various musical keys.</li><li>He describes the key of E flat major as expressing \"devotion.\"</li><li>\"Superstition\" (1972) by Stevie Wonder is a song written in E flat major.</li></ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain how Schubart describes E flat major. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>In <em>Ideas Toward an Aesthetic of Music</em>, Schubart describes the mood of E flat major as expressing \"devotion.\"</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>The key of E flat major is one of various keys, which are sets of musical notes that form the foundations of pieces of music.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>The song \"Superstition\" could be described as expressing \"devotion.\"</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>In <em>Ideas Toward an Aesthetic of Music</em>, Schubart describes the mood of E flat major.</p>"}],"correct":"A","rationale":""},{"number":"1","section":"Reading and Writing","module":2,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In a 2020 paper, Arya Udry et al. cautioned that although similarities in the isotopic signatures of elements detected in Mars’s atmosphere and in Martian meteorites recovered on Earth make it tempting to treat the geochemical properties of the meteorites as ______ blank those of Mars’s interiors, Mars’s geology cannot be ascertained based solely on meteorite-sample analyses.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>contrivances of</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>catalysts of</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>proxies for</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>deterrents to</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"2","section":"Reading and Writing","module":2,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>The following text is adapted from Virginia Woolf's 1919 novel <i>Night and Day</i>. Katharine is the granddaughter of a celebrated poet.</p><p>[Katharine’s] descent from [a celebrated poet] was no surprise to her, but matter for satisfaction, until, as the years wore on, certain drawbacks made themselves very manifest. Perhaps it is a little depressing to inherit not lands but an example of intellectual virtue; perhaps the conclusiveness of a great ancestor is a little discouraging to those who run the risk of comparison with him.</p>","stem":"<p>As used in the text, what does the word “manifest” most nearly mean?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Involved</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Expected</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Particular</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Evident</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"3","section":"Reading and Writing","module":2,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Danza Azteca arose in the 1500s when the traditions of the Pame people ______ blank those of other tribes in Central Mexico, creating a hybrid dance form that spread across Mexico and later to Mexican communities in the US. Pame influences can still be seen in the performances by groups like Danza Azteca Huitzilopochtli, which is based in Woodburn, Oregon.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>corresponded to</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>advocated for</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>deferred to</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>intermingled with</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"4","section":"Reading and Writing","module":2,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>As the use of non-oil energy resources has increased, the relationship between oil shocks—such as the 9% rise in oil prices from January to February of 1957—and national economic activity has ______ blank. Indeed, Gbadebo Oladosu and colleagues showed that the effect of recent oil shocks on the gross domestic product of the United Kingdom was only slightly greater than zero.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>oscillated</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>reverted</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>acquiesced</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>abated</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"5","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>The Federalist Papers are a collection of 85 essays written by Alexander Hamilton, John Jay, and James Madison. They were published pseudonymously in the <i>Independent Journal</i> and other New York newspapers in 1787–88 and argue that New Yorkers should vote to ratify the proposed United States Constitution. Though the authorship of most of the individual essays is certain, that of a few is in question: for instance, while No. 8, \"The Consequences of Hostilities between the States,\" was surely penned by Hamilton, No. 62, “The Senate,\" may have been written by either Hamilton or Madison.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>To describe a collection of essays and address what is known about their authorship</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>To point out that some essays in a notable collection were written by one person, while others were written by two people</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>To summarize a contentious argument made in a collection of essays</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>To present the publication history of a collection of essays</p>"}],"correct":"A","rationale":""},{"number":"6","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>The following text is from Anne Spencer's 1922 poem \"Translation.\"</p><p>We trekked into a far country,<br>My friend and I.<br>Our deeper content was never spoken,<br>But each knew all the other said.<br>He told me how calm his soul was laid<br>By the lack of <u>anvil and strife</u>.<br>“The wooing kestrel,” I said, \"mutes his mating-note<br>To please the harmony of this sweet silence.”</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the reference to \"anvil and strife\" in the text as a whole?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>It represents a strong contrast to the speaker's friend's current experience of tranquility.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>It symbolizes the speaker's friend's view that meaningful work and social engagement are core components of a fulfilling life.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>It illustrates how the speaker and her friend understand each other well even without being explicit about their feelings.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>It emphasizes a difference between the natural world and the experience of the speaker and her friend.</p>"}],"correct":"A","rationale":""},{"number":"7","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>The Museum of Modern Art (MOMA) in New York City has an exhibition of video games that includes <i>Space Invaders</i> from 1978, which museum visitors can play on site, and <i>The Sims</i> from 2000, which visitors can see only in a video presentation. <mark>MOMA claims the video presentations are only for games that would be impractical to display in a playable form, but video games are an inherently interactive medium, a feature that is grossly absent in a video-only presentation.</mark></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>It provides MOMA's rationale for a decision that the author critiques.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>It introduces a fact about video games that the author thinks MOMA did not appropriately consider when choosing which video games to exhibit.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>It presents a misconception about video games that the author believes is evident in MOMA's choice about which video games to exhibit.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>It details a criticism of MOMA that the author defends against in the remainder of the sentence.</p>"}],"correct":"A","rationale":""},{"number":"8","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Studies of the population density of cougars (<p>Puma concolor</p>) have yielded a range of results, which may in part reflected differences in the effectiveness of the methods that that researchers have used in their studies. If, for example, the use of biopsy darting and snow backtracking tends to lead to overestimates of cougar population density, that may explain why the study by ___________ blank</p><p>[IMAGE: Page 41 - Table titled 'Studies of Cougar Population Density' with columns for Study authors, Location, Methods, Study area (square kilometers), and Maximum density (cougars per 100 square kilometers).]</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the example?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Juan I. Zanón-Martinez et al. required a study area of 1,179 square kilometers, while the study by Robin E. Russell et al. required a study area of 1,225 square kilometers.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Robin E. Russell et al. reported a maximum density of 6.70 individuals per 100 square kilometers, higher than that reported by several other studies.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Randy D. Johnson reported a maximum density of only 2.78 individuals per 100 square kilometers, lower than that reported by several other studies.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Gregory A. Davidson et al. reported a maximum density of 5.50 individuals per 100 square kilometers, while the study by Juan I. Zanón-Martinez et al., which used regular camera trapping, reported a lower maximum density.</p>"}],"correct":"B","rationale":""},{"number":"9","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Autofiction is an emerging literary form that incorporates autobiographical writing into fiction. Practitioners of autofiction emphasize the autobiographical aspects of their work by patterning their protagonists' lives after their own, typically naming their protagonists after themselves. Autofiction usually borrows formal elements from autobiographical writing too, including its first person point of view and its use of a narrative voice so forthright that it borders on confessional. Ocean Vuong's 2019 novel <p>On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous</p> is a representative work of autofiction, one that erases the distinction that fiction typically observes between authors and the characters they create.</p>","stem":"<p>Which statement, if true, would most strongly support the text's description of <p>On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous</p>?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p><p>On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous</p> makes use of imagery in much the same way that contemporary poetry does, exploiting certain images—butterflies, for example—for their emotional or conceptual associations; in this way, Vuong blurs the distinction between poetry and fiction.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>The novel portrays the upbringing of the protagonist, a young writer, in a working-class Vietnamese American family that is virtually identical to Vuong's family, and the protagonist narrates the novel in a tone that many reviewers describe as surprisingly frank.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Although the narrative of <p>On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous</p> proceeds in a largely chronological fashion, as narratives do in most works of fiction, the form of Vuong's narrative is unique: the novel consists of a single long letter that the protagonist, a soft-spoken young man, has written to his mother.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>The novel's protagonist bears an obvious resemblance to Vuong himself, and in interviews Vuong speaks of the protagonist as a sort of alter ego, yet the protagonist refers to himself as “Little Dog” throughout the novel and never divulges his actual name.</p>"}],"correct":"B","rationale":""},{"number":"10","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>A tariff is a tax on imported goods intended to protect domestic producers of similar goods from international competition. Eliminating tariffs can lead to an influx of cheaper imported goods, lowering prices; in a place where domestic production is relatively expensive, this influx can suppress domestic production, as the country's consumers favor more cheaply produced imported goods over domestically produced ones. A student consults a table showing projected changes in production and average market prices of agricultural commodities in four countries in a tariff-elimination scenario. Based on the data, the student claims that compared with China and Russia, agricultural production in Argentina and Canada is likely relatively inexpensive.</p><p>Projected Percent Change in Agricultural Production and Market Price under Tariff-Elimination Scenario</p><table><thead><tr><th>Country</th><th>Percent change in total production</th><th>Percent change in market prices</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Argentina</td><td>+0.90</td><td>+1.02</td></tr><tr><td>Canada</td><td>+3.74</td><td>+0.84</td></tr><tr><td>China</td><td>-0.03</td><td>-0.11</td></tr><tr><td>Russia</td><td>-3.48</td><td>-0.99</td></tr></tbody></table>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses information from the text and data from the table to support the student's claim?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Although eliminating tariffs would likely cause agricultural market prices to decrease in most locations, it would likely cause agricultural market prices to increase in both Argentina and Canada.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Argentina and Canada are the only countries shown that are projected to see both an increase in agricultural production and a reduction in agricultural prices in the absence of tariffs.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>In the absence of tariffs, total agricultural production in Argentina and Canada would likely exceed that in China and Russia.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Although agricultural production in China and Russia would likely decrease if tariffs were eliminated, it would likely increase in both Argentina and Canada.</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"11","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Fracture Toughness Values for CrMnFeCoNi and CrCoNi at Various Temperatures</p>[IMAGE: Page 47 - graph of fracture toughness vs. temperature for different materials and types of toughness]<ul><li>The following 4 lines are shown:</li><li>CrCoNi initiation toughness</li><li>CrCoNi propagation toughness</li><li>CrMnFeCoNi initiation toughness</li><li>CrMnFeCoNi propagation toughness</li><li>The CrCoNi initiation toughness line:</li><li>Begins at 20 kelvins, 459</li><li>Falls sharply to 77 kelvins, 280</li><li>Falls gradually to 198 kelvins, 278</li><li>Falls gradually to 293 kelvins, 215</li><li>The CrCoNi propagation toughness line:</li><li>Begins at 20 kelvins, 544</li><li>Falls sharply to 77 kelvins, 458</li><li>Falls gradually to 198 kelvins, 456</li><li>Falls sharply to 293 kelvins, 400</li><li>The CrMnFeCoNi initiation toughness line:</li><li>Begins at 20 kelvins, 262</li><li>Falls gradually to 77 kelvins, 223</li><li>Falls gradually to 198 kelvins, 222</li><li>Falls gradually to 293 kelvins, 220</li><li>The CrMnFeCoNi propagation toughness line:</li><li>Begins at 20 kelvins, 383</li><li>Falls sharply to 77 kelvins, 310</li><li>Rises gradually to 198 kelvins, 322</li><li>Falls gradually to 293 kelvins, 304</li></ul>","stem":"<p>Which choice best describes the overall trend in the fracture toughness of CrMnFeCoNi?</p>","keySource":"solved","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Its initiation toughness increases steadily with increasing temperature, while its propagation toughness decreases.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Both its initiation and propagation toughness values generally decrease as the temperature increases, with some fluctuations.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Its initiation toughness remains relatively stable across all temperatures, but its propagation toughness shows a significant increase at higher temperatures.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Both its initiation and propagation toughness values increase as the temperature decreases.</p>"}],"correct":"B","rationale":"<p>Looking at the descriptions for CrMnFeCoNi initiation toughness and propagation toughness:<ul><li>CrMnFeCoNi initiation toughness: Begins at 20 kelvins (262), gradually falls to 77 kelvins (223), gradually falls to 198 kelvins (222), and gradually falls to 293 kelvins (220). This is a general decrease.</li><li>CrMnFeCoNi propagation toughness: Begins at 20 kelvins (383), falls sharply to 77 kelvins (310), rises gradually to 198 kelvins (322), and falls gradually to 293 kelvins (304). This shows an overall decrease with a slight rise in the middle.</li></ul>Both values generally decrease as temperature increases, with some fluctuations (like the slight rise in propagation toughness). Therefore, option B is the best description.</p>"},{"number":"12","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p><em>The Clouds</em> is a 423 BCE play by Aristophanes, originally written in ancient Greek. At the time, professional intellectuals called sophists taught customers rhetorical techniques to use in public speaking, along with providing instruction in other subjects. In the play, Aristophanes satirizes sophists as having an exaggerated sense of their own wisdom, as seen when the character ______.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses a quotation from a translation of <em>The Clouds</em> to illustrate the claim?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Strepsiades encourages his son to learn to be a sophist, saying, \"But go as quickly as possible and learn instead of me.\"</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>Strepsiades says of a sophist business, “There dwell men who in speaking of the heavens persuade people that it is an oven, and that it encompasses us, and that we are the embers. These men teach, if one give them money, to conquer in speaking, right or wrong.”</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>Socrates, a sophist, says to a customer, \"Do not then always revolve your thoughts about yourself; but slack away your mind into the air.\"</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Socrates, a sophist, says to a new customer, \"Come, then, take care that, whenever I propound any clever dogma about abstruse matters, you [seize] immediately.\"</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"13","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>A film studies student is researching early 20th-century film serials, which consisted of individual episodes of a single long story that were shown weekly in theaters. <em>The Mystery Squadron</em> is a 1933 serial that, over its 12 episodes, kept its audience interested with the suspense and drama that are typical of the aviation genre. The student, however, claims that ultimately audiences of the time preferred resolution and closure over ongoing tension.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student's claim?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>Modern critics generally regard the first episode as the best installment of <em>The Mystery Squadron</em>.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>The 12th episode of <em>The Mystery Squadron</em> was the most expensive episode of the series to produce.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>The 12th episode of <em>The Mystery Squadron</em> was viewed by more people than was any previous episode in the series.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>Audiences of the time considered <em>The Mystery Squadron</em> to belong to a genre other than the aviation genre.</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"14","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Paleoanthropologists have struggled to determine the onset of consistent clothing use by hominids. The appearance of hide-scraping tools by 780,000 years ago (780 Ka) enabled clothing production but does not entail it, and while clothing is thought to have been thermally superfluous for anatomically modern humans in their African range around their emergence by 300 Ka, it was essential for the persistence of humans outside Africa by 70 Ka. Melissa Toups et al. proposed a novel proxy: the evolution of the clothing-exclusive variant of the human-exclusive parasite <em>Pediculus humanus</em> (head lice), which Toups et al. date to as early as 170 Ka. This variant could not have emerged and survived without ample ecological niches, so if Toups et al. are correct, then ______.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>the spread of regular clothing use among humans after approximately 70 Ka may have been motivated by factors other than the thermal benefits that clothing provides.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>humans most likely began regularly wearing clothing at some point after 170 Ka but before approximately 70 Ka.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>it is unlikely that hominids other than anatomically modern humans produced or wore clothing even though tools enabling the production of clothing existed well before the emergence of humans.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>a substantial proportion of humans must have regularly worn clothing long before humans permanently inhabited areas cold enough to necessitate doing so.</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"15","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In biology, many initially disordered systems will naturally move toward greater order according to the principle of self-organization, a conceptual framework for pattern ______ biologists believe can be used to explain a range of organic phenomena, from population dynamics to the sociality of bees and ants.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>formation. Some</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>formation that some</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>formation, that some</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>formation; some</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"16","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Many contemporary Indigenous painters practice a specifically Indigenous mode of abstraction; for example, Linda Lomahaftewa often assembles compositions out of motifs common in the ceramics and other traditional arts of the Hopi Tribe. In contrast, the prominent Indigenous practitioners of abstract painting during the mid-twentieth century, such as the Ojibwe artist George Morrison, typically aligned their compositional strategies with Abstract Expressionism—a school of painting dominated by European American artists—instead of with traditionally nonrepresentational forms of Indigenous art. Thus, in the case of Morrison’s generation, the identification of an abstract painting as Indigenous art tends to ______ blank.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>place a greater emphasis on the artist's biography than on the aesthetic merit of the painting.</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>obscure the Indigenous origin of certain motifs associated with Abstract Expressionism.</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>deny the extent to which cultural identity influences an artist's work.</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>depend not on stylistic details but instead on an awareness of the artist's identity.</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"17","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Dong Lai and Diego J. Muñoz's work on circumbinary disk accretion—a process in which, due to gravity, material from an orbiting disk spirals inward and accumulates onto two stars or black holes—relies heavily on simulations. Lai and Muñoz recognize the need for direct ______ blank recommending evolving binary star systems as a promising avenue for future study.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>observation; however,</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>observation. However,</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>observation, however,</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>observation, however;</p>"}],"correct":"C","rationale":""},{"number":"18","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>Conceptual artist Joan Jonas’s first work, <i>Wind</i> (1968), is a silent film depicting people on a beach struggling to move about in extremely windy conditions. <i>Wind</i> has been recognized for bringing the principles of postmodern dance—which demystifies traditional choreography by foregrounding features of movement that ballet and other classical dance forms obscure (such as clumsiness, weightiness, resistance, and ______ blank to video.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>gravity),</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>gravity);</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>gravity)</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>gravity)—</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"19","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In a 2018 study, Ceri Shipton et al. concluded that stone tools excavated from an archaeological site in Saudi Arabia date to between 197,000 and 276,000 years ago. The reliability of the dates that Shipton et al. have posited, which were obtained through processes that determined when buried mineral grains surrounding the tools were last exposed to sunlight, ______ blank in part upon the researchers' exclusion of mineral grains that lacked adequate sun exposure before burial.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>depends</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>depend</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>having depended</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>depending</p>"}],"correct":"A","rationale":""},{"number":"20","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In recent years, the English writer Helen Maria Williams (1759–1827), whose poetry was widely read during her lifetime, has been rediscovered by contemporary audiences—a resurgence of interest that is largely due to literary scholars such as John Bugg, whose work ______ blank the richness of Williams's poems and the poet's historical importance to the Romantic literary movement encourages a broader rethinking of British Romanticism itself.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>is highlighting</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>has highlighted</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>highlights</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>highlighting</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"21","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In 1977, legendary Puerto Rican performer Rita Moreno won an Emmy Award, making her one of the rare talents to earn the highest honors in television, music, film, and stage entertainment—the Emmy, Grammy, Oscar, and Tony ______ blank the achievement is known as \"winning the EGOT.\"</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","keySource":"paper","choices":[{"label":"A","content":"<p>awards; respectively</p>"},{"label":"B","content":"<p>awards, respectively,</p>"},{"label":"C","content":"<p>awards, respectively—</p>"},{"label":"D","content":"<p>awards, respectively;</p>"}],"correct":"D","rationale":""},{"number":"22","section":"Reading and Writing","module":1,"format":"mc","difficulty":"M","stimulus":"<p>In a given rock formation, Roadian rock from 273 million years ago might directly abut Tortonian rock from 11.6 million years ago. But how